{"reply":"In **Chapter 3, “A Caucus-Race and a Long Tale,”** the Dodo announces:

> “_Everybody_ has won, and all must have prizes.”

When asked who should give the prizes, he says:

> “Why, _she_, of course,” said the Dodo, pointing to Alice with one finger;

The thimble feels awkward because the “prize” is Alice’s own object, taken from her pocket and ceremonially handed back to her. The ceremony makes that circularity especially conspicuous: the Dodo says, “We beg your acceptance of this elegant thimble;” and Alice thinks the whole thing is absurd while trying to look as solemn as everyone else.

That seems close to what you describe about the thank-you lunches: the gratitude may be genuine, but the form of it makes the effort feel circular. You have already supplied the deposit and the organising; being thanked can then feel less like receiving something new and more like having your own contribution ceremonially returned. Does that awkwardness fit better as a problem with the thanks themselves, or with the fact that your original effort is still quietly being treated as yours to provide?","evidence_uses":[{"supported_claims":["In **Chapter 3, “A Caucus-Race and a Long Tale,”** the Dodo announces:","“_Everybody_ has won, and all must have prizes.”","When asked who should give the prizes, he says:","“Why, _she_, of course,” said the Dodo, pointing to Alice with one finger;"],"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch03-ln0553-0587","source_location":"Chapter 3 — A Caucus-Race and a Long Tale, source lines 553-587","exact_quote":"“_Everybody_ has won, and all must have prizes.”"},{"supported_claims":["The thimble feels awkward because the “prize” is Alice’s own object, taken from her pocket and ceremonially handed back to her.","The ceremony makes that circularity especially conspicuous: the Dodo says, “We beg your acceptance of this elegant thimble;” and Alice thinks the whole thing is absurd while trying to look as solemn as everyone else."],"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch03-ln0578-0617","source_location":"Chapter 3 — A Caucus-Race and a Long Tale, source lines 578-617","exact_quote":"“We beg your acceptance of this elegant thimble;”"}],"memory":{"kind":"no_memory_candidate","reason_code":"automatic_capture_disabled"}}

